hi everyone i'm annie john baptiste and  i lead product inclusion at google  product inclusion is all about bringing  an inclusive lens throughout the product  design process and I'm excited to share  a little bit about how we've been doing  that first I just want to welcome you to  the session this may be a newer concept  for you and so I hope that it's helpful  but I'm super excited that you're  spending the time to learn about product  inclusion today before we get started I  just wanted to level-set and have a  shared framework around what we mean  when we say product inclusion product  inclusion is about building designing  coding for everyone so no matter who you  are where you live what you look like we  want to make sure that you feel thought  of in the entire product design process  and when you pick up a product or  service and so at Google we want to make  sure that we're bringing that inclusive  lens throughout everything that we  create so the first thing that's really  important to think about is really how  this ties back into Google's mission and  so we talked about organizing the  world's information and making it  universally accessible and useful it's  really the universal peace that we're  leaning into in product inclusion and we  say not just for some for everyone right  so I talked a little bit about some  dimensions that we think of but it's  really important to make sure that  everyone is thought of in the process  and especially those who have been  historically underrepresented in the  product design process now this work  builds on a lot of great work namely  accessibility work and next billion  users work what happens with product  inclusion is that we actually look at 12  dimensions of diversity and the  intersections of those dimensions what I  mean when I say the intersections of the  dimensions is that users aren't in a  silo  right they don't have one thing that  makes them who they are and so it's  really important to think about  everything holistically when you're  building products or services using  myself as an example I'm a black woman  I'm also left-handed I'm not black on  Monday a woman on Tuesday and  left-handed on Wednesday all of those  things are encompassed within me affect  how I move through the world and  definitely affect how I use product  and so we want to make sure that we're  thinking holistically about all of those  dimensions on when we're building so on  this slide you're seeing a few of the  dimensions that we look at again we look  at 12 but things like gender age ability  education socioeconomic status and more  are really integral in to making sure  that you're asking who else and really  broadening who your users could and  should be it's also really important to  note that there's a lot of untapped  opportunity when you bring an inclusive  lens and really Center those who have  been historically underrepresented so  what you're seeing on this slide are a  bunch of statistics around things like  purchasing power digital usage for a lot  of the historically underrepresented  groups that we focus on with product  inclusion so you can see stats as it  relates to accessibility right there 1  billion people in the world with a  disability for example or you can look  at 700 million users coming online in  the next few years it's really exciting  that there's so much opportunity when  you bring in inclusive lens and that can  really affect your business and being  able to serve even more users it's  important to note that product inclusion  is not just a one point in your process  it's really throughout the process and  the earlier that you bring it in the  better so you can bring it in an  ideation for example when you're  starting to think of a product or  service that may have an untapped  opportunity but what's really important  is to highlight and understand what key  points in the process you're going to  kind of bring that inclusive lens we  call those inflection points and on this  slide you can see there are multiple  inflection points where we've started to  bring that inclusive lens throughout the  process and the goal is that the end  result is more inclusive more people so  next I'm just going to jump into a few  case studies to help you understand what  our product in gee inclusion journey  looks like and how this can actually be  put into action so this first video is  from a team that worked on camera  sensors and what they wanted to do was  really ensure that they brought this  inclusive lens and ensured all skin  tones were accurately represented what I  love about this is that it shows that no  matter where you are in the organization  should you have a part to play in  building for everyone and with everyone  so with that we'll roll the video  pictures tell stories it doesn't really  matter if it's a selfie or if it's a  portrait that somebody is taking of  another person you are sharing in that  human moment let's take a picture you  too  one two three oh he's too dark so this  is where the discussion started if you  just use my phone it's got a better  sensor  better sensor what does that even mean  color tuning is an extremely complicated  process when we look at images when we  try to do is figure out how much of a  difference there is between let's say a  reference image and the image that we  actually are trying to quantify we were  running some tests on a product and it  was the proximity sensor we were testing  and we said oh it looks like it does 60  centimeters and we look to each other  and go we're both white the technology  itself as many people will say is not  racist it's just that it wasn't tested  properly to make sure that the designers  weren't unconsciously biased hey there's  an entire world out there and we want to  make sure this works for everybody I  love the fact that we get to influence  cameras that we get to share those  experiences of life and emotion to me  that is what imaging is about it is a  vehicle to express humanity I take a  picture of you two  one two three the next case study I'd  like to show you is with duo low light  mode what the duo low like team did was  and be intentional about bringing  diverse perspectives to the table from  the beginning of their process at Google  we have what we call inclusion champions  who are Googlers from across the globe  in multiple dimensions of diversity who  have helped us dog food or user test our  products so the duo low light mode team  knew that they wanted to make sure that  no matter where you are in the world or  if you had certain lighting or you had  light or dark skin tones you are going  to be accurately represented when using  this video product and so they worked  with our inclusion champions to make  sure that we tested across multiple  dimensions of diversity to make sure  that again everyone feels beautifully  and accurately represented and thought  of when they use the product now I'll go  into a little bit of the research that  we've been doing over the past year on  the business case for inclusion I hope  it's helpful to start to frame what  users and  they're thinking about as lates to  products inclusion but also how you can  start to implement it in your everyday  work so the first piece of data that I  want to share with you all is that there  are four places in the product design  process where it's really important to  bring that inclusive lens those four  points are the ideation user research  user testing and marketing phases now a  few slides back I showed you an  end-to-end product inclusion process  slide where you saw multiple inflection  points or points in the process where  you could bring product inclusions into  the fold that being said when we did  research and looked at a bunch of  different teams across Google what we  found was that these are the four points  where teams constantly came back to and  also these were the four points where if  you did bring an inclusive lens were  intentional about doing so you actually  disproportionately created a more  inclusive product and so would encourage  you all to think about if these four  points in the process make sense for  your organization and how you can bring  that inclusive lens to those four points  it's also important to just start to ask  who else so for example if you're  starting to id8 within your organization  maybe bring someone from another piece  of the organization or another team into  the brainstorming because they'll likely  have different perspectives if you're  already doing user research maybe you do  user research internationally or with a  different demographic than you usually  do right these small changes can lead to  really big results as it relates to  bringing with that inclusive lens the  second piece was that a hundred percent  of teams that brought an inclusive lens  into their process did so at at least  two of the four touch points I just  mentioned right so either the ideation  user research user testing or marketing  phases they did at least two so it may  be the ideation and marketing or the  user research and user testing but they  made sure that it wasn't just an  isolated point in time but that they  were doing it throughout the process and  I think that that's really important not  only because you're starting to think  holistically about your process but also  you can involve different team members  and different organization members who  have different expertise to again start  to bring more perspectives that have  historically been underrepresented into  the fold  another key finding was that both  underrepresented and majority groups  prefer inclusive marketing and product  design what does that mean it's been a  notion that only underrepresented groups  want to see themselves reflected and  what we found is that that's actually  not true right everyone or the majority  of people want to see diversity and  inclusion both in marketing and  storytelling but also in product design  and so I think that that's a big shift  that we want to make sure that we think  about it's not just those are who have  been historically underrepresented but  it's actually underrepresented and  majority consumers or users the last  finding that I'd like to share with you  all is that teams no matter their  representation that were intentional  about bringing an inclusive lens were  able to do so right so regardless of the  team makeup and we talked a lot about  you know the systemic changes that need  to happen in order to have  representation and inclusive culture  that is incredibly important still but  even as you're working to get to that  representation you can start to work on  product inclusion now right so  regardless of the makeup if you're  setting standards if you're holding  yourself and your team accountable you  can actually start to make headway in  building that inclusive product so now  I'm just going to go through three  principles that we help to guide teams  along their product inclusion journey  the first one is to address the user so  I showed you two a few of the twelve  dimensions that we focus on as it  relates to product inclusion but really  thinking about what dimensions have been  underrepresented in your process and  bringing those into the fold you want to  address the users again asking who else  and continuing to expand the pool of who  users could and should be the second one  is to start with equity so inclusion  shouldn't be an afterthought it's not  something that comes in after you've  actually created everything right how  can you bring it in earlier on in the  process and at multiple points in the  process again this doesn't sit within  one team it sits within all parts of the  organization and so how are you making  sure that you found those key process  points and you brought the key  stakeholders in to kind of help move  that along  and the last one is to continually 